    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `customerCode` now follows `<REGION>-<HASH8>` and everything derived from it moves to the new identity \u2014 while touching only **two small policy classes**.\n\n**`OwnerCustomerCodePolicy`** \u2014 rewrote `assignCustomerCode` to build `region(postcode) + \"-\" + hash8(owner)`:\n- REGION derived from the postcode range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099, else `UNKNOWN`).\n- HASH8 = first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName`.\n- Dropped the per-city sequence numbers and the owner-count scan entirely. Kept the `clinicService` parameter so the controller call site is untouched.\n\n**`OwnerLocalityPolicy`** \u2014 `locality` now reads the REGION component straight off the `customerCode` (the identity), rather than independently re-deriving from postcode/city. Removed the now-redundant region/postcode lookup tables.\n\n## What I deliberately left alone\n\n- **`OwnerMembershipPolicy`** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`), **`OwnerCheckDigitPolicy`** (Luhn over the code's digits), and **`OwnerAuditPolicy`** (logs `customerCode`) all already read from `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so they inherit the new region-and-hash identity for free \u2014 no edits, no extra files touched.\n\nThis keeps structural impact minimal: two edits, both in small single-purpose policy classes (near-zero `WMC_other`), no changes to the larger controller, mapper, or service. The audit line from the test run confirms the new form: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.",
